Problem Statement: Predicting Customer Purchase Behavior

A retail company wants to understand customer behavior to improve marketing campaigns and increase sales.
The company has historical data about its customers, including demographics, income, online activity,
and past purchases.

The goal is to build a predictive model that estimates the likelihood or value of a customer’s purchase.

This problem can be approached as:

Regression → Predict the purchase amount (continuous value).

Classification → Predict whether the customer will make a purchase (Yes/No).
========================================================================================
1. Define the Objective

Regression → Predict customer purchase amount.

Classification → Predict purchase decision.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Dataset Description

Features (X): Age, Gender, Annual Income, Website Visits, Time on App, Previous Purchases, Campaign Responses, etc.

Target (y):

Continuous (₹ purchase amount) for regression.

Binary (Yes/No purchase) for classification.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Data Preprocessing

Handle missing values.

Encode categorical variables (Label/OneHot Encoding).

Scale/normalize numerical features if required.

Split dataset into training and test sets.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Model Selection

Apply one or more of the following algorithms:

Linear Regression (continuous target).

Logistic Regression (binary classification).

Decision Tree (both regression & classification).

Random Forest (both regression & classification).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Model Training

Train the chosen model(s) on the training dataset.

Tune hyperparameters (e.g., max_depth, n_estimators).

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Model Evaluation

For Regression: RMSE, MAE, R².

========================================
| Algorithm               | Type           | How It Fits the Problem                                       | Pros                                           | Cons                                       |
| ----------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| **Linear Regression**   | Regression     | Predicts purchase amount (₹).                                 | Simple, interpretable.                         | Assumes linearity, sensitive to outliers.  |
| **Logistic Regression** | Classification | Predicts probability of purchase (Yes/No).                    | Probabilistic, interpretable.                  | Limited with non-linear data.              |
| **Decision Tree**       | Both           | Splits customers based on rules to predict spend or purchase. | Easy to explain, captures non-linear patterns. | Can overfit without tuning.                |
| **Random Forest**       | Both           | Uses multiple trees for more robust predictions.              | High accuracy, handles complex data.           | Less interpretable, computationally heavy. |
